{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{4x}+Bx{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6{e}^{4x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $4$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =\\alpha (2x+4{x}^{2}){e}^{4x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (2+16x+16{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (2+16x+16{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}-8\\alpha (2x+4{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}+16\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha (2+16x+16{x}^{2}-16x-32{x}^{2}+16{x}^{2}){e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =6 \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+Bx{e}^{4x}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}.\n$$" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186088949 1064 "Given a second-order ODE with exponential forcing $f(x) = ae^{bx},$ if $e^{bx}$ and $xe^{bx}$ are *both* terms in the complementary solution, then neither can be a particular solution. Instead, we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2 e^{bx}.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the solution to the following ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 12\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 36y = e ^{6x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)(\\lambda -6) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = 6$ is a repeated root. Consequently, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{6 x} + Bxe^{6 x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $e^{6x},$ and $e^{6x}$ and $xe^{6x}$ are both terms in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2 e^{6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =6\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{6x}+2\\alpha x{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =12x\\alpha {e}^{6x}+36\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{6x}+2\\alpha {e}^{6x}+12\\alpha x{e}^{6x} \\\\  & =36\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{6x}+2\\alpha {e}^{6x}+24\\alpha x{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$  we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\underline {36 \\alpha x^2 e ^{6x}}\n+2 \\alpha e ^{6x} +\\underline{ 24 \\alpha x e ^{6x} }-12(\\underline{6 \\alpha x^ 2e ^{6x} }+ \n\\underline {2 \\alpha x e^{6x}})\n+\\underline{36  \\alpha x^2 e ^{6x}} = e^{6x}.\n$$\n\nCanceling the underlined terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha {e}^{6x} & ={e}^{6x}\\,\\Rightarrow \\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{1}{2} x^2 e^{6x},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{c} \\\\  & =A{e}^{6x}+Bx{e}^{6x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534692 17592186045417 false] [17592186088949 1064 "Given a second-order ODE with exponential forcing $f(x) = ae^{bx},$ if $e^{bx}$ and $xe^{bx}$ are *both* terms in the complementary solution, then neither can be a particular solution. Instead, we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2 e^{bx}.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the solution to the following ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 12\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 36y = e ^{6x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)(\\lambda -6) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = 6$ is a repeated root. Consequently, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{6 x} + Bxe^{6 x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $e^{6x},$ and $e^{6x}$ and $xe^{6x}$ are both terms in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2 e^{6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =6\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{6x}+2\\alpha x{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =12x\\alpha {e}^{6x}+36\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{6x}+2\\alpha {e}^{6x}+12\\alpha x{e}^{6x} \\\\  & =36\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{6x}+2\\alpha {e}^{6x}+24\\alpha x{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$  we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\underline {36 \\alpha x^2 e ^{6x}}\n+2 \\alpha e ^{6x} +\\underline{ 24 \\alpha x e ^{6x} }-12(\\underline{6 \\alpha x^ 2e ^{6x} }+ \n\\underline {2 \\alpha x e^{6x}})\n+\\underline{36  \\alpha x^2 e ^{6x}} = e^{6x}.\n$$\n\nCanceling the underlined terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha {e}^{6x} & ={e}^{6x}\\,⇒\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{1}{2} x^2 e^{6x},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{c} \\\\  & =A{e}^{6x}+Bx{e}^{6x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432960 1080 "y=A{e}^{-5x}+Bx{e}^{-5x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432960 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432960 1083 #uuid "13784b02-b4fa-5428-8054-3023ffb7b9cf" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432961 1080 "y=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{5x}-x{e}^{5x}" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432961 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432961 1083 #uuid "16229b38-60cb-5780-8ad0-917d7c6f87c9" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432962 1080 "y=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-4x}-{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432962 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432962 1083 #uuid "17d07888-62a7-5089-85be-41fe6c4f2fbb" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432963 1080 "y=x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{3x}" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432963 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432963 1083 #uuid "449d3531-77be-5f66-9133-bd8cb60e1ed8" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432964 1080 "y={e}^{2x}-x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{3x}" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432964 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432964 1083 #uuid "58c93f78-afb5-5ee8-96a0-a85936bf2dd9" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432965 1080 "y=A{e}^{4x}+Bx{e}^{4x}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432965 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432965 1083 #uuid "aacaccd9-fea2-5f32-9106-999e883a5424" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432966 1080 "y={e}^{3x}" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432966 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534692 17