13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432977 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y={e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y={e}^{3x},\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432977 1072 #uuid "2168103f-a07e-527a-b5ab-cb35206d23bf" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432977 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{2(0)}+B(0){e}^{2(0)}+{e}^{3(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A+1 \\\\ A & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2A{e}^{2x}+2Bx{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the derivative we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2A{e}^{2(0)}+2B(0){e}^{2(0)}+B{e}^{2(0)}+3{e}^{3(0)} \\\\ 2 & =2A+B+3 \\\\ -1 & =2A+B \\\\ -1 & =2(0)+B \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A=0$ and $B=-1,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny=-x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{3x}\n$$" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432975 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432978 1069 "q-93688" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432978 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=8{e}^{5x}.\n$$" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432978 1072 #uuid "6369e1a3-96fe-518c-bd64-2e83f3ab9685" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true] [17592186432978 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =5$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8{e}^{5x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $5$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =\\alpha (2x+5{x}^{2}){e}^{5x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (2+20x+25{x}^{2}){e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (2+20x+25{x}^{2}){e}^{5x}-10\\alpha (2x+5{x}^{2}){e}^{5x}+25\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha (2+20x+25{x}^{2}-20x-50{x}^{2}+25{x}^{2}){e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =8 \\\\ \\alpha  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}.\n$$" 13194139534692 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-13784b02-b4fa-5428-8054-3023ffb7b9cf" 17592186432960 "answer-16229b38-60cb-5780-8ad0-917d7c6f87c9" 17592186432961 "answer-17d07888-62a7-5089-85be-41fe6c4f2fbb" 17592186432962 "answer-449d3531-77be-5f66-9133-bd8cb60e1ed8" 17592186432963 "answer-58c